#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e305e84-e33c-59c1-bd3f-086453fc6408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382060} {:answer/id #uuid "a8267c92-6ff8-541c-b37c-708844918388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382061} {:answer/id #uuid "46ab690e-260e-530c-bbb2-16bacaa8c0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382062} {:answer/id #uuid "275c2438-59f4-5507-8f62-29a6b159d1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382063} {:answer/id #uuid "c76e0e71-d112-50c4-97a1-8bec0a6ab774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "275c2438-59f4-5507-8f62-29a6b159d1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b4e78-d785-58a4-ae44-4f6bd088fed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a9407fa-0156-51d0-868b-a6bb68a8909c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140498" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0325e8-8b6c-586d-b83b-d336b14159f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382067} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c55d19-794a-5188-8bae-067da7ce929d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} {:answer/id #uuid "d20a6bb5-547c-5c0d-81f1-591bf52af8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382069} {:answer/id #uuid "049eb50a-4d0c-5bd3-b9a4-b1c9d6b6f4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382070} {:answer/id #uuid "da98d65a-6485-5302-a2fa-bb9cfbed7d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20a6bb5-547c-5c0d-81f1-591bf52af8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382069} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e5f5d4b-ae66-5809-9811-870151c8154f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b71065be-44ce-5f43-8458-63be59f4de8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140501" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8530e221-8480-5e7f-9ee3-5ddc194261fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382073} {:answer/id #uuid "66e288fc-e9b2-57de-97d1-51040cee93a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382074} {:answer/id #uuid "d41e0dbd-8b93-5799-9904-9f8c482026a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382075} {:answer/id #uuid "8579bc1b-e4fb-5450-8669-30bdd453ed70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} {:answer/id #uuid "a51ec88d-4fc9-52ad-8ef5-6d3461c5ed83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8579bc1b-e4fb-5450-8669-30bdd453ed70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d78d26-89a1-519b-b709-ac6b559a349a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53d94693-fca2-5635-a557-72030e989666" :question/math-academy-id "q-39161" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "922cb98c-f013-533b-a6d2-86acfd8be69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382080} {:answer/id #uuid "389bdac7-003e-50b0-b3b2-957bb05dff1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382081} {:answer/id #uuid "095ac666-2859-585c-92d5-7ff621a2962c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382082} {:answer/id #uuid "3d33cf77-8304-5e85-b136-33eede1e7352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382083} {:answer/id #uuid "955f9959-4c4f-55d3-bb2f-89d90311e7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d33cf77-8304-5e85-b136-33eede1e7352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382083} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea42d253-d02a-526e-81d5-5ef1934d7630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc4a04e7-d61f-575a-ad59-bf6415c59c4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39166" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=4\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4-6\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=4-6\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4-6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6\\text{i} & -3 \\\\ 15 & -3+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 15{x}_{1}+(-3+6\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee56d75-5298-5909-b3f9-11a9ea0825b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382087} {:answer/id #uuid "38f4ace1-d05e-5081-8c4c-133378e8474e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382088} {:answer/id #uuid "349c06d1-4681-57c7-96ee-7c5f3fd4b116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382089} {:answer/id #uuid "4566c5d2-45fc-52c3-a17a-cc61888d2440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382090} {:answer/id #uuid "96c3292b-4ff8-5700-a1ff-bf6149740b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f4ace1-d05e-5081-8c4c-133378e8474e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382088} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e5d541-2198-505d-b488-4599ff53d288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5da7514b-8a73-5a50-bb87-67043a1bc3f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99342" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
